    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192741": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-track Position in Africana Studies",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Tenured/Tenure-track Position in Africana Studies University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Africana Studies Location Philadelphia, PA Open Date Sep 08, 2026 Description The Department of Africana Studies at the University of Pennsylvania seeks a historian of sub-Saharan Africa at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The successful candidate will be selected based on superb scholarship and outstanding teaching and mentoring and will join a department distinguished by a clear commitment to Global Black Studies as an interdisciplinary endeavor. Overview of the Department & the School of Arts & Sciences The Department of Africana Studies is an interdisciplinary department devoted to the critical and systematic examination of the cultural, political, social, economic, and historical experiences of peoples of African descent around the world, including North America, Africa, Latin America, the Caribbean, Europe, and the Middle East. The Department is committed to a rigorous, cooperative, and cross-regional approach that brings fresh perspectives to the study and teaching of a new Global Black Studies. The Department works closely with the Center for Africana Studies , a research hub for scholars across the campus doing intellectual work that highlights the impact of Africa and the African diaspora on humanity. We are housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported as much by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. The Department offers a major and minor for undergraduate students, a graduate certificate program, and a Ph.D. Our curriculum represents a broad range of disciplines and thematic interests, including: Political Theories and Movements; Cultural and Literary Studies; Arts and Performance; Gender and Sexuality; Social Justice; and History. Ours is a unique approach to the study and teaching of Global Black Studies, with a dedication to training the next generation of scholars in this field. We are especially interested in historians whose research and teaching focus on religious and/or sociocultural practices in sub-Saharan Africa. Qualifications include All candidates should have: Publications appropriate to rank Demonstrable commitment to teaching undergraduate and graduate students, and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body in Africana Studies Expertise/training in the history of sub-Saharan Africa and its relationship with the African diaspora Active research agenda, preferably in religion and/or socio-cultural practices in Africa Ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research for a diverse student body Willingness to provide service in the department and school, and to assume leadership positions in the Department and Center as appropriate Candidates are expected to have the PhD in hand by July 1, 2027. Application instructions/Required documents: Please provide the following application materials: CV Cover letter that includes a description of Research, Teaching, and Mentoring A writing sample of no more than 30 pages—could be a published article, book chapter, chapter of a manuscript, or chapter of a dissertation. Contact information for no more than three referees. Additional supporting materials may be requested at a later date. We will begin reviewing applications by October 16, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Pennsylvania is an equal opportunity employer. Candidates are considered for employment without regard to race, color, sex, sexual orientation, religion, creed, national origin (including shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics), citizenship status, age, disability, veteran status or any class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. or its licensors and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. Support | Careers | Accessibility Policy | Cookies Settings | Privacy Policy | Terms of Service We use cookies that are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyze, improve, and personalize our content and your digital experience. You can manage your cookie preferences using the Cookie settings link. For more information, see our Cookie Policy Cookie settings Accept all cookies Opt-Out Request Honored Cookie Preference Center We use cookies which are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyse, improve and personalise our content and your digital experience. For more information, see our Cookie Policy and the list of Google Ad-Tech Vendors . You may choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types may impact your experience of our site and the services we are able to offer. See the different category headings below to find out more or change your settings. You may also be able to exercise your privacy choices as described in our Privacy Policy Allow all Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you 