    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194060": {
      "title": "Apply",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Faculty Positions in the Division of Addiction Medicine University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Family Medicine and Community Health Location Philadelphia, PA Open Date Sep 24, 2026 Deadline Sep 24, 2028 at 11:59 PM Eastern Time Description The Division of Addiction Medicine within the Department of Family Medicine and Community Health at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for multiple faculty positions in Addiction Medicine in the non-tenure Academic Clinician (AC) track. Successful candidates will hold an M.D. or D.O. degree and must have primary board in a recognized ABMS or AOA Specialty and must be board certified in Addiction Medicine. They should have experience in hospital-based addiction medicine consultation and in underserved and vulnerable patient care and an interest in a career in academic medicine. Preferred candidates will have completion of an Addiction Medicine fellowship; experience in inpatient consultation services; experience caring for patients with opioid use disorder and other substance use disorders; experience working in Bridge/OTP or low-barrier treatment programs. Teaching responsibilities may include participation in the education and supervision of residents, fellows, and medical students in inpatient, consultative, ambulatory, and Bridge/OTP settings. Opportunities for educational leadership and program development may be available for the right candidate. Clinical responsibilities may include clinical care based primarily at Pennsylvania Hospital, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, HUP Cedar, Bridge/Opioid Treatment Services (OTP) services, and other Penn Medicine-affiliated clinical settings. Faculty may participate in ambulatory addiction medicine, Bridge/OTP services, treatment of opioid and other substance use disorders, hospital-based addiction treatment, community-based addiction services, resident and fellow supervision, and multidisciplinary care coordination. All faculty members will devote a portion of their clinical effort to inpatient Addiction Medicine consult services at one or more Penn Medicine hospitals, including Pennsylvania Hospital, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, and HUP Cedar. The Division of Addiction Medicine is recruiting for three full-time faculty positions that will support Penn Medicine's growing addiction medicine clinical, educational, and community programs. These positions offer flexibility in clinical practice location and scope while supporting access to evidence-based addiction treatment across Penn Medicine. The Division of Addiction Medicine serves diverse patients across Philadelphia, including many individuals and communities that have historically experienced barriers to health care access and health equity. We seek candidates who demonstrate a commitment to caring for medically and socially complex patient populations and who have experience working in underserved communities and resource-limited clinical settings. Academic Clinician faculty are not required to maintain an independent research program; however, scholarly activity, quality improvement, curriculum development, and educational innovation are encouraged and supported. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement The University of Pennsylvania is an equal opportunity employer. Candidates are considered for employment without regard to race, color, sex, sexual orientation, religion, creed, national origin (including shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics), citizenship status, age, disability, veteran status or any class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. or its licensors and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. Support | Careers | Accessibility Policy | Cookies Settings | Privacy Policy | Terms of Service We use cookies that are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyze, improve, and personalize our content and your digital experience. You can manage your cookie preferences using the Cookie settings link. For more information, see our Cookie Policy Cookie settings Accept all cookies Opt-Out Request Honored Cookie Preference Center We use cookies which are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyse, improve and personalise our content and your digital experience. For more information, see our Cookie Policy and the list of Google Ad-Tech Vendors . You may choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types may impact your experience of our site and the services we are able to offer. See the different category headings below to find out more or change your settings. You may also be able to exercise your privacy choices as described in our Privacy Policy Allow all Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Cookie Details List‎ Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and